00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:49.090
Hundreds of thousands of lines of vital software code is still under development.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.900
Manage every function of the X-planes.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.970
That work gets tested here in a multi-million...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.120
simulator. Boeing's lead test pilot, Fred Knox, puts the foe fighter through its

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:14.800
paces. Modern fighters are designed to be

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:21.450
aerodynamically unstable. Under computer control, that aerial volatility

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:29.890
transforms into acrobatic agility. Every simulated

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.480
Flight by Knox helps refine this essential software.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:40.260
Flight control software controls the airplane the way it flies,

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.440
but it also turns on the air conditioner.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.730
It raises and lowers the landing gear.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:46.650
It navigates for us.

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.870
It does every critical element and every critical safety element in the airplane.

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:52.750
If we haven't done the development here, the airplane will not fly.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.980
But less than two months after rollout, the software...

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.280
development suddenly goes offline. Boeing is crippled by the largest white-collar

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:12.230
strike in American history. 17,000 aerospace engineers are off the job,

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:20.160
including more than a hundred developing the X-Plane's flight controls. Progress

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:26.930
inside the Boeing Phantom Works grinds to nearly a halt, while outside a small

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:28.650
group of engineers joins the strike.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:34.330
for everybody you know everybody really has real mixed emotions I think and is

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:41.880
real conflicted about it 40 days later the strike ends Boeing doesn't escape

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:48.650
unscathed the strike on our programs is a terrible wound we lost weeks of

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:55.230
schedule those weeks will not be recovered with the setbacks Boeing's

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.580
lead over Lockheed evaporates

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.720
After years of jousting back and forth, these two combatants are galloping toward the tournament grounds,

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:10.390
toward the arena where X-planes and test pilots meet their fate.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:15.600
It's time for this battle to take to the air.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:28.620
Just 30 miles away from the skunk and phantom works lies the proving grounds for all of America's X-planes.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.060
Edwards Air Force Base is a tremendous facility,

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.500
and one of the hallmarks of that facility is the lake bed.

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:46.800
It's about 12 miles wide, it's 20 miles long,

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:48.780
and it's a very hard, flat surface.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:51.120
And you can put the airplane down,

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.600
and you don't have to worry about running out of runway

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:55.360
because you've got the whole lake bed in front of you.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:59.580
With these wide-open spaces...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.100
Edwards and experimental planes go back to the first supersonic flights.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:09.330
Here, the original X-Plane, the X-1 flown by Chuck Yeager,

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:12.550
broke the sound barrier over half a century ago.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.820
Since then, aviation triumphs and tragedies

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:23.230
have made Edwards the hallowed ground of the X-Plane history.

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:29.970
Now, these skies will hold an epic contest never seen before.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.780
before, a battle between X-planes.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.720
Historically, we have 47 X-plane programs.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:40.240
This is the first time in history, ever,

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:44.080
that any two of those X-airplanes have competed

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:46.960
against each other for a production contract.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:48.480
It's unprecedented.

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:53.690
It's time for Boeing's dream to take flight,

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.600
while Lockheed can only watch from the ground.

00:06:58.570 --> 00:06:59.890
After years of derision,

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:06.940
As a second-rate contender, Boeing proves even an underdog, like its X-32, can have its day.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.720
This is Freddy Knox from the Boeing Company.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:14.240
We're getting ready to launch the X-32 on his first flight this morning.

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:18.780
And I wonder if I can get a little forecast for the wind, say, from about 7.30...

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:22.840
Fred Knox, Boeing's chief test pilot and a key developer of the X-plane,

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.220
will take the craft on its maiden flight.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:27.420
I appreciate your help this morning. Bye-bye.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:28.960
Good day to go.

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:35.990
With the fate of the Boeing effort resting on his shoulders,

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:39.490
Knox receives a final blessing from Frank Statkis.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:40.810
See you at the other end.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:41.250
Absolutely.

00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:46.470
For me, it's about as big a day as a test pilot's ever going to have.

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:49.260
A chance to go do a first flight, it's a big day for me,

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:50.620
it's a big day for the rest of the team.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:53.780
We spent four years now working very hard,

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:57.100
everybody from flight controls to APU pumps to structure

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.980
and a lot of hard work, and we should get a nice safe flight.

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:05.220
It's a big day for all of us.

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:08.330
See you guys at Edwards!

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:15.460
Yeah, couldn't be a nicer day.

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:21.650
I'm excited, I'm bummed.

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:23.000
We're ready to go.

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:26.100
Everybody's smiling, look at that.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:27.400
You're looking sharp, sir.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.920
F-8 forever, you bet.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:37.350
If Fred Knox is nervous, he doesn't show it.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:40.670
Even after finding some stray tools in the cockpit.

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:47.110
Home sweet home!

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:04.680
It'll be fun. Thank you, sir.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:13.300
Knox is alone in the plane. He has plenty of company in the air.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:18.780
Two chase planes flown by other test pilots will monitor his flight.

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:29.970
It's going to be exciting. Finally, all of our life is in there.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:46.700
Let's sweat and tears like proud parents the weary engineers and mechanics of the phantom works gather to see their fighter off

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:04.700
Today's flight isn't a round trip.

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:07.870
The Boeing plane is leaving the Phantom Works for good.

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:12.710
Take up residence at Edwards Air Force Base, a short distance away.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:41.400
I was bawling like a baby, yeah, I mean, it was excitement, you know,

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:44.180
and then it was just kind of mainly a huge sense of relief.

00:10:50.590 --> 00:10:54.020
Within minutes, the X-Plane is in the airspace over Edwards,

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:56.090
wheels down just in case.

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:04.410
Knox has flown this plane for hundreds of hours in a simulator.

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:08.320
Now he gets to see if the real thing handles the same way.

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:12.230
11-alpha 160 looks good.

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:14.090
Hey, I'm happy with the airplane.

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:19.630
Then, Boeing test pilot Dennis O'Donohue in his chase plane spots a problem.

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:27.360
On the starboard side, you've got hydraulic pollutant leaking from about the forward mid-slash.

