I'm just guessing this is the first slide.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.300
light stuff going out a little bit, but we'll watch it.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:36.140
I'll keep an eye on it.

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:36.730
Roger.

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:39.250
With the source of the leak uncertain,

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.380
Knox is told to cut short his long-awaited flight.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.220
Test points and we'll set up for a landing here.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:51.060
We'll find way 2-5, we'll land 2-5-0, full temperature 3-0-0-2,

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:52.420
clear to the Hauptbahn airport.

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:53.480
We have found a little bit your favorite.

00:11:57.730 --> 00:11:58.070
We got in.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:07.280
There she is, got one flight under her belt.

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:09.110
We dropped a little fluid out of it.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:10.740
We never lost it. Everything stayed up.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:11.680
It's a full normal landing.

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:14.320
The flying quality is about 11.

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:15.790
O'Donohue was getting nervous.

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:16.990
He couldn't stand the fluid pumping out.

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:19.600
So it's time to land, huh?

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:21.020
Yeah, it was definitely time to land.

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:23.680
From the moment I saw it, it was time to land.

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:26.000
The hydraulic leak turns out to be minor,

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:29.320
a tiny glitch in an otherwise triumphant day.

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:38.100
Over Edwards, Boeing begins a series of test flights.

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:45.780
During each one, the pilot puts the plane through a specific set of maneuvers known as test points.

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:49.820
Sensors document the plane's flying characteristics.

00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:52.040
The results go to the JSF.

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:57.970
So far, the plane's performance closely mirrors the Boeing simulations.

00:12:57.970 --> 00:12:58.770
Assigned.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.140
of just how sophisticated computer design has become.

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:12.180
Every pilot has been astounded at how closely the airplane

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:13.620
actually matches what we thought it would do,

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:16.580
from air speeds and flying qualities and system performance.

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:19.120
That's just been a really good surprise.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:23.760
While this version of the Boeing X-Plane

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:25.920
is intended for both the Air Force and Navy,

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:29.500
the Navy requirements that will be the most demanding.

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:37.150
Commander Phil Yates, call sign Rowdy, is the official Navy test pilot assigned to the Boeing effort.

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:40.670
For him, it was an unexpected honor.

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:46.220
I received a phone call, how would you like to be the first Navy pilot to fly the JSF?

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:51.270
After picking my chin up off the ground, I said, yeah, I think I'd like to do that.

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:59.980
Carrier landings are a testament to precision skill of Navy pilots, and Rowdy is one of the best.

00:14:04.570 --> 00:14:06.910
In preparation for testing the Boeing X-plane,

00:14:07.460 --> 00:14:10.090
he takes an F-18 Hornet out for a spin.

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:15.820
Okay, good nozzles, good hydraulic pressure, good RPM.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:18.950
Here's a salute. Here we go.

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:39.020
Carrier landings are probably the most demanding task a pilot may be faced with,

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:44.000
especially at night in adverse conditions, pitching deck, bad weather.

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:49.570
You have to be able to precisely control the airplane.

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:53.760
Flying at about 150 miles per hour,

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:57.850
Navy pilots aim for a target zone of only 120 feet,

00:14:58.500 --> 00:14:59.980
about the size of a tennis court.

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:03.720
they must catch one of four arresting cables.

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:12.320
Pilots don't apply brakes.

00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:14.850
In fact, at contact with the flight deck,

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:16.900
they gun the engine to full power,

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:19.140
so that if the plane misses the cables,

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:21.020
there's enough thrust to get airborne.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.720
Landing on a runway is like threading a belt through a belt loop.

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:36.500
Landing on a carrier is like threading a needle.

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:41.880
A test pilot's job is to jump into a plane in which he may have little experience

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:44.340
and report on its pros and cons.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:47.920
When you start doing that in an airplane that's never been flown before,

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:52.800
it really is what gets a test pilot, I think, excited.

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:59.660
What's exciting to a test pilot would be sheer terror to most people.

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:04.060
Here at Edwards, Rowdy will put Boeing's X-Plane

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:06.300
through the precision maneuvers of a carrier landing.

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:13.940
We all recognize that these are unproven airplanes.

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:16.960
But we as test pilots deal with that,

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:20.780
that we're going to be able to handle any situation

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:23.070
that the aircraft presents to us.

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:26.080
If we don't feel that way, we wouldn't be flying.

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:39.440
The Boeing team has worked hard to minimize the danger.

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:45.380
Test requires Rowdy to fly so close to the ground,

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:48.510
any error or technical problem may be fatal.

00:16:48.790 --> 00:16:56.440
A section of runway has been marked off, equal to the landing strip on a carrier deck.

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:11.540
From a control room miles away, a team of Boeing engineers monitors the X-plane's every move.

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:21.980
The pilot learns what kind of corrections and control inputs he has to make,

00:17:22.120 --> 00:17:26.020
and then it's also the aircraft's ability to respond to those control inputs.

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:29.960
It's that combination that ultimately determines how well the air...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:37.370
plane is going to do at the ship. As he would on a real carrier, Rowdy receives

00:17:37.370 --> 00:17:43.420
visual cues from an optical landing aid called the Fresnel lens. If he can line up an amber

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:48.180
light called the meatball correctly, Rowdy knows he's approaching at a safe angle for

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:55.060
a successful touchdown. He gets additional tips from a landing signal officer on the

00:17:55.060 --> 00:17:59.970
ground. A real carrier, this officer would give us

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:07.180
score to every landing we're not really trying to grade the pilot on what what

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:10.680
Rowdy's doing he's a skilled aviator who knows how to make the corrections

00:18:12.290 --> 00:18:16.480
Raj ball so we're trying to look is find out how the airplane is performing with

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:26.730
certain deviations applied to it each attempt the degree of difficulty goes

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:29.750
up to recreate

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.220
real-world conditions. Rowdy begins an approach descending too fast or at too

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:49.070
steep an angle and then tries to correct for it. The X in X-Plane means

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:57.030
experimental but occasionally it means unexpected. On one test flight pilot

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:59.050
Dennis O'Donoghue runs into trouble.

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:05.620
We were just doing a routine test with the aircraft to see if we ever lost the engine.

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:08.240
Could we crank the engine back up and get it relit?

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:14.810
Suddenly a warning light comes on indicating the X-Plane's landing brakes have failed.

00:19:31.310 --> 00:19:33.010
Suspecting the warning light is at fault,

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:35.780
O'Donohue brings the plane in to land on the runway.

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:38.880
Without brakes, he will quickly run out of room,

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:42.120
risking injury to himself and his reputation.

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:45.600
Wrecking a multi-million dollar X-plane

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:47.370
doesn't look good on the resume.

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:53.240
Touchdown, press the brake pedals, no response.

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:56.260
It was just a matter of adding power and getting airborne again.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:04.160
With the brakes definitely gone, it's time for Plan B,

00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:09.840
saving a 21st-century plane using a 2-million-year-old lake bed.

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:13.340
Plenty of lake bed in front of me.

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:17.990
Touched down and just let the aircraft roll to a stop.

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:28.740
Had we not had the lake bed, that would have been a much more critical emergency.

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:29.880
Much more critical.

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:37.230
After a month of successful flights, Boeing's luck has run out.

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:41.780
Repairing the brakes reveals a major software problem.

00:20:45.340 --> 00:20:46.340
Planes grounded.

00:20:48.890 --> 00:20:54.740
To make matters worse, Boeing no longer has the skies over Edwards to itself.

00:20:59.380 --> 00:20:59.980
That very...

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:05.490
Every day, Lockheed's X-Plane is finally ready to leave its factory home and head into battle.

00:21:09.070 --> 00:21:15.460
The X-Plane's first flight is in the hands of Chief Test Pilot Tom Morgenfeld.

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:22.990
Having flown everything from the first stealth fighter to black aircraft that are still classified,

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:26.310
Morgenfeld has unrivaled experience.

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:33.370
Yet he is all too familiar with the dangers of flight tests.

00:21:35.130 --> 00:21:38.550
In 1992, while piloting an Air Force prototype,

00:21:39.340 --> 00:21:41.980
a computer malfunction sends Morgenfeld's aircraft

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:43.980
into a violent oscillation.

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:55.100
After skidding in flames for more than a mile,

00:21:55.840 --> 00:21:57.840
Morgenfeld walks away unharmed.

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:07.590
Now, nearly a decade later, the legendary test pilot is about to climb into another untested fighter.

00:22:07.940 --> 00:22:10.420
No turning back now, I think. I've committed myself, huh?

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:15.540
First time you fly an airplane, it's a tremendous thrill.

