which it didn't have time to build,

00:43:40.150 --> 00:43:42.790
but will submit to the JSF as its final proposal,

00:43:43.290 --> 00:43:44.950
is 1,500 pounds lighter.

00:43:50.740 --> 00:43:52.580
Dennis O'Donohue is in the cockpit again,

00:43:53.190 --> 00:43:55.330
while flight test conductor Howard Gophis

00:43:55.680 --> 00:43:57.720
closely monitor the mission from the ground.

00:43:58.560 --> 00:43:59.980
Now, there's few...

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.740
were unknowns we know we can do it we know we've been there we know what we've

00:44:02.740 --> 00:44:06.660
seen so far but we're still only one failure away from having a really bad

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:16.380
day closing in over the pit the Boeing x-plane comes to a stop begins a slow

00:44:16.380 --> 00:44:25.990
descent disaster strikes O'Donohue is now too low to eject

00:44:30.570 --> 00:44:30.990
He's over.

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:37.240
Suddenly, the controllers spot trouble.

00:44:40.240 --> 00:44:45.290
Invisibly, the engine has ingested hot gas from the lift nozzles and loses power.

00:44:47.550 --> 00:44:51.050
O'Donohue feels the bottom dropping out. It's too late to abort.

00:45:00.510 --> 00:45:03.940
20 feet and only seconds from the ground, the gas dissipates,

00:45:04.270 --> 00:45:06.910
and the engine gains enough thrust to touch down safely.

00:45:08.760 --> 00:45:09.560
Excellent landing.

00:45:10.390 --> 00:45:12.270
He's down on the ground, and we realize it,

00:45:12.780 --> 00:45:17.370
and so there's the, you're in the quandary for that split second.

00:45:17.530 --> 00:45:20.350
Okay, we just did our first VL. What happened?

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:26.740
Over by the runway, no one is aware of the close call.

00:45:30.860 --> 00:45:37.650
Reviewing the data, the test team believes a choice made to increase safety, the hover pit, may be causing the problem.

00:45:38.870 --> 00:45:41.530
There's almost no crosswind to clear the pit of exhaust.

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:45.760
Hot gas may be collecting and bouncing upward into the air.

00:45:49.560 --> 00:45:53.840
They decide to attempt a second vertical landing, but on a solid surface.

00:45:54.020 --> 00:45:59.980
We decided we were going to go for the vertical.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:06.470
landing on the path so we set up all the numbers set it all up and and know that

00:46:06.470 --> 00:46:16.940
the hate the same thing could happen there the second vertical landing goes

00:46:16.940 --> 00:46:28.750
without a hitch everyone's enormous relief just a week later during another

00:46:28.750 --> 00:46:29.530
vertical landing

00:46:30.350 --> 00:46:32.490
An old friend pays an unwelcome visit.

00:46:34.800 --> 00:46:41.320
It's a pop stall, the result of hot gas ingestion just above the ground, common event in Harriers.

00:46:42.140 --> 00:46:45.960
Boeing engineers predicted it might happen and designed it out of their new version.

00:46:46.930 --> 00:46:50.430
But they decide to play it safe and stop testing their vertical system.

00:46:54.800 --> 00:46:59.960
A month later, Boeing completes all major requirements for the Pentagon, ahead of the

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:06.980
competition it's a major landmark and if anything has them worried the Boeing

00:47:06.980 --> 00:47:11.780
executives certainly don't show it felt great it looked great it felt great yeah

00:47:11.780 --> 00:47:19.880
I'm confident that we are the head of the class now and I expect to stay there

00:47:30.860 --> 00:47:34.570
The Lockheed plane now needs to prove it's ready for prime time

00:47:34.570 --> 00:47:41.430
by performing the critical transition from conventional flight to hover to landing vertically.

00:47:42.660 --> 00:47:46.560
We need to demonstrate that we can land on a solid surface,

00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:49.660
both to make sure we've got the performance and the flying qualities to do that,

00:47:49.800 --> 00:47:54.320
to make sure that we've dealt with ground effects, such as hot gas ingestion,

00:47:54.640 --> 00:47:59.440
and to prove that we can land on a normal sort of surface.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:12.180
out damage or significant erosion to the surface a thousand feet simon hargraves engages the lift

00:48:12.180 --> 00:48:18.260
fan and slows down with air from the front and exhaust from the rear nozzle in balance

00:48:18.260 --> 00:48:26.640
the lockheed x-plane floats on nearly 40 000 pounds of thrust this system avoids the problems

00:48:26.640 --> 00:48:28.680
of the harrier boeing's direct lift

00:48:30.160 --> 00:48:33.300
Cooler air from the lift fan creates an invisible barrier

00:48:33.300 --> 00:48:36.540
that prevents the engine from choking on its own hot gas.

00:48:43.500 --> 00:48:47.680
After two minutes of hovering, Hargraves eases off the throttle

00:48:47.680 --> 00:48:50.020
and gently guides the X-plane down.

00:48:50.940 --> 00:48:52.600
Beautiful. No problems at all.

00:49:15.470 --> 00:49:29.900
It's been a long time coming and that's only I can say is yes it's gonna be a tough

00:49:30.030 --> 00:49:35.370
choice if one guy had stumbled here at the end then it would have made it easy it's not an easy

00:49:35.370 --> 00:49:39.250
choice and that's what the government wanted the government wanted the close horse race and i think

00:49:39.250 --> 00:49:47.540
they're going to get it in the waning days of the competition at an undisclosed location somewhere

00:49:47.540 --> 00:49:55.250
near the pentagon jsf director general michael how takes nova inside a world where cameras have

00:49:55.250 --> 00:49:59.760
never been allowed. Behind a wall of security,

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:05.150
The secret proposals of Boeing and Lockheed are being evaluated by the government team

00:50:05.150 --> 00:50:08.760
that will help determine the winner of the Joint Strike Fighter program.

00:50:10.240 --> 00:50:12.820
This is where the proposals are, all electronic, of course.

00:50:13.390 --> 00:50:17.370
This is where we've got 200 people off and on coming in to look at the proposals one at a time,

00:50:17.950 --> 00:50:20.250
gauging them against the operational requirements document.

00:50:21.430 --> 00:50:27.700
Digging through mountains of data, experts evaluate performance, cost, management, and risk.

00:50:28.600 --> 00:50:29.960
Some of them are doing aerodynamic performance.

00:50:30.760 --> 00:50:36.220
figuring out how fast will it go, what is the range, how will it turn, what kind of G's can it sustain.

00:50:36.670 --> 00:50:38.770
Others are evaluating software and architecture.

00:50:39.650 --> 00:50:46.090
Now, near the end of this jury process, the results are one of JSF's most closely guarded secrets.

00:50:46.310 --> 00:50:49.690
We've got about six weeks left by which we're going to take the results of our evaluation,

00:50:50.180 --> 00:50:53.140
give it to the Secretary of the Air Force, who in harmony with the Secretary of the Navy

00:50:53.140 --> 00:50:57.270
is going to make a decision of who's going to build the airplane for the warfighter for the next 40 years.

00:51:00.640 --> 00:51:04.600
As the final decision approaches, known in military-speak as Down Select,

00:51:05.560 --> 00:51:08.560
Boeing remains confident that its manufacturing know-how

00:51:08.560 --> 00:51:11.580
and cost-saving designs have made it a winner.

00:51:12.370 --> 00:51:15.210
I think we all feel pretty good about going into Down Select,

00:51:15.290 --> 00:51:19.310
and I think we truly believe that we've got the right vehicle for the customer.

00:51:21.980 --> 00:51:24.080
But just before it crosses the finish line,

00:51:24.630 --> 00:51:29.350
Lockheed plans a final dramatic display, a bid for the history books.

00:51:30.190 --> 00:51:32.940
and bait for the huge government contract.

00:51:36.050 --> 00:51:38.450
In a test flight Lockheed dubs Mission X,

00:51:39.170 --> 00:51:41.630
its fighter takes off in less than 500 feet,

00:51:43.100 --> 00:51:48.970
then goes supersonic and lands vertically.

00:51:56.600 --> 00:51:59.960
Since the Harrier is subsonic, the maneuver is a mile.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:05.770
stone in aviation history, and a direct hit on Boeing's need to strip off parts for vertical

00:52:05.770 --> 00:52:15.070
landing and reinstall them for supersonic flight. But the Lockheed team pushes its luck

00:52:15.070 --> 00:52:26.300
too far. They attempt a vertical takeoff and transition to conventional flight. Plane bobbles

00:52:26.300 --> 00:52:28.930
in the wind on liftoff. The mission is aborted.

00:52:31.720 --> 00:52:35.420
But the failure does nothing to dampen Lockheed's legendary mix

00:52:35.420 --> 00:52:38.460
of technical ingenuity and engineering arrogance.

00:52:39.640 --> 00:52:42.020
This company believes it has won the right

00:52:42.020 --> 00:52:45.060
to build the first fighter of the 21st century.

00:52:45.770 --> 00:52:47.210
We did our part of the bargain.

00:52:47.680 --> 00:52:49.120
Now the rest of it's up to the government.

